    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, quick question for you. We just bought a new CNC milling machine for the shop. It cost us $85,000, and we're estimating it'll have a useful life of 10 years, with a salvage value of $5,000 at the end of that. Can you walk me through how the straight-line depreciation calculation works for this so I can get it into the year-end reports correctly?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Of course, I'm happy to help. To calculate the annual depreciation expense using the straight-line method, you first subtract the salvage value from the initial cost to get the depreciable base. Then, you divide that by the useful life of the machine. For your CNC mill, you would subtract $5,000 from $85,000, resulting in a depreciable base of $80,000. Next, you divide $80,000 by the 10-year useful life, which gives you an annual depreciation expense of $8,000. So you will report an annual depreciation expense of $8,000 on your year-end reports for this machine.",
      "source": "target",
